    "created_at": "2025-10-08T07:30:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-10-08T07:30:11Z",
    "body": "> Would it make sense to move them all into one algorithm that runs any time any element is popped off the stack of open elements?\n\nPresumably this is what WebKit (and I guess Chromium) call `finishParsingChildren()`? It seems reasonable to let elements define such steps and then the parser pops we run those steps for the relevant element, if it has any defined. (Or they have them always defined but by default no-op.)\n\nGenerally the existence of this mechanism is a bit of a design smell and cannot be replicated by custom elements as it would make it very easy to design elements that work declaratively, but not imperatively. I'm not sure how to convey that properly however.\n\n> I suppose that it's not perfect unless we also instrument every place that pops elements off the stack of open elements.\n\nRight, I think we want to make \"popping\" an algorithm.\n\n> Does this have an explicit step which pops elements off the stack which we could instrument?\n\nYeah I think so. \"Stops parsing\" and \"abort a parser\" both pop.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
